.TH VM 3
.SH NAME
vm \- the Dis virtual machines, one a core
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #X /dev

.B /dev/vm/ctl
.BI /dev/vm/ n /run
.BI /dev/vm/ n /status
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The native kernel runs more than one Dis virtual machine: up to one a
processor core, numbered from 0.
Each has its own interpreter, heap, garbage collector and module table,
so a program in one VM runs at the same time as a program in another, on
a different core.
VM 0 is the one the kernel boots and the desktop runs in; the others are
made the first time something is run in them.
.PP
VMs share the kernel and nothing else: the namespace, devices, files,
pipes and served names are the same for all of them, but a Dis object \(em
a channel, an array, a reference of any kind \(em belongs to one VM and
cannot be passed to another.
Programs in different VMs therefore talk the way programs on different
machines do, through files.
.I Spawn
stays in its VM; a program is placed in another only by
.BR run .
.PP
.B Ctl
reads
.BI "vms " started " max " m
\(em how many VMs exist and how many there can be.
.PP
Writing a command to
.BI vm/ n /run
runs it in VM
.IR n :
the first word is the path of a Dis module, the rest are its arguments,
quoted as by
.IR tokenize (2).
The module's
.B init
is called with a nil context and the words as its argument list; a module
whose
.B init
does not take those is refused.
The new process has the writer's namespace, environment and file
descriptors, shared as a
.B spawn
shares them, and is given a heap in VM
.IR n .
A read of the same open file returns its pid.
.PP
.BI vm/ n /status
reads
.EX
	vm \fIn\fP progs \fIp\fP heap \fIused\fP \fImax\fP
.EE
or, for a VM not yet made,
.BI "vm " n " not started" .
.PP
.IR Ps (1),
.IR kill (1)
and
.IR prog (3)
see the processes of every VM.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a program in VM 2 by hand (or use
.IR vm (1),
which does the same):
.EX
	echo /dis/myserver.dis -p 8080 >/dev/vm/2/run
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/port/devvm.c
.br
.B /os/port/dis.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR vm (1),
.IR prog (3),
.IR osinit (8)
.SH BUGS
.B $Tk
and
.B $Draw
are loaded only in VM 0: their state assumes one interpreter.
.br
A program written to
.B run
from a process whose standard output is that same file has that file as
its standard output too, which is rarely wanted;
.IR vm (1)
opens
.B run
on a descriptor of its own for that reason.
.br
VMs are not destroyed, and there is no way to move a running process from
one to another.
